SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Frame this domain as the review's live-debate endpoint: (1) the
metabolic-rift/world-ecology dispute (Foster/Burkett/Saito vs. Moore, with
Malm and Gellert as adjudicators) as a direct continuation of the
base/superstructure and technological-determinism debates found in the
Cohen/analytical-Marxism and Political Marxism domains, now conducted over
nature rather than class; and (2) the Chibber/postcolonial-theory dispute
as a continuation of the Eurocentrism critique found in the
world-systems/dependency domain, now conducted over historical
materialism's claim to theorize a single, universalizing capitalist
world-history.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Metabolic rift (nature/society as really distinct but equally material):
  6 papers (Foster 1999, Burkett 1999, Saito 2017, Saito 2023, Foster and
  Burkett 2016, Malm 2019) -- capitalism ruptures a determinate metabolic
  interchange between humanity and nature that Marx's mature political
  economy already theorized.
- World-ecology / hybridist critique: 1 paper (Moore 2017) -- rift thinking
  smuggles in a Cartesian dualism; capitalism must be theorized as a
  "world-ecology" internal to nature, not acting upon it from outside.
- Synthesis/adjudication of the rift-vs-ecology dispute: 1 paper (Gellert
  2018) -- compares metabolic rift, world-ecology, and ecologically
  unequal exchange as partially overlapping, partially rival frameworks.
- Defense/reconstruction of historical materialism as a general theory of
  history and social change: 3 papers (Chibber 2011, Chibber 2013, Prew
  et al. 2018) -- historical materialism's core categories remain
  defensible and empirically productive after the collapse of both
  technological-determinist orthodoxy and Marxism-Leninist state power.
- Critical reception of the post-1990s defenses: 1 paper (Lazarus 2016) --
  contests Chibber's universalizing defense of capital's categories against
  postcolonial theory.
====================================================================
}

@article{foster1999metabolicrift,
  author = {Foster, John Bellamy},
  title = {Marx's Theory of Metabolic Rift: Classical Foundations for Environmental Sociology},
  journal = {American Journal of Sociology},
  year = {1999},
  volume = {105},
  number = {2},
  pages = {366--405},
  doi = {10.1086/210315},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Foster reconstructs Marx's late engagement with Justus von Liebig's critique of capitalist "robbery" agriculture to argue that Marx theorized a "metabolic rift": capitalism's town/country division of labor systematically severs the material exchange (metabolism) between human beings and the soil, exporting nutrients embodied in food and fiber to the city and rupturing natural cycles of soil replenishment. Foster presents this not as a peripheral aside but as integral to Marx's mature critique of capitalist agriculture in Capital, Volume 3.

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of ecological Marxism as a distinct reconstruction of historical materialism, establishing that Marx's theory of history already contained an account of humanity's material relation to nature that later Marxist orthodoxy (and productivist readings of the "development of the productive forces") had suppressed or ignored. It is the reference point against which Saito's MEGA-based extensions, Moore's world-ecology critique, and Malm's defense of dualism are all positioned.

  POSITION: Founding statement of the "metabolic rift" school of ecological Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, metabolic-rift, Foster, High},
  abstract = {This article addresses a paradox: on the one hand, environmental sociology, as currently developed, is closely associated with the thesis that the classical sociological tradition is devoid of systematic insights into environmental problems; on the other hand, evidence of crucial classical contributions in this area, particularly in Marx, but also in Weber, Durkheim, and others, is too abundant to be convincingly denied. The nature of this paradox, its origins, and the means of transcending it are illustrated primarily through an analysis of Marx's theory of metabolic rift, which, it is contended, offers important classical foundations for environmental sociology.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{burkett1999marxnature,
  author = {Burkett, Paul},
  title = {Marx and Nature: A Red and Green Perspective},
  publisher = {St. Martin's Press},
  year = {1999},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Burkett argues that Marx's value theory itself, not merely his scattered remarks on agriculture, presupposes and depends on nature as a material substratum of production: labor's ability to create value and use-value alike rests on nature's provision of "free gifts" (raw materials, energy, natural fertility) that capital appropriates without cost, so that treating capitalist growth as unconstrained by natural limits is a mystification internal to the value form itself, not something Marx's political economy overlooked.

  RELEVANCE: Burkett supplies the systematic, value-theoretic complement to Foster's metabolic-rift thesis, together establishing the twin textual and theoretical basis on which subsequent ecological-Marxist reconstructions of historical materialism (Saito, Foster and Burkett's joint later work) build; it directly engages -- and rejects -- the charge from earlier eco-socialist critics (e.g., Ted Benton) that Marx's productivism is incompatible with ecological limits.

  POSITION: Value-theoretic grounding of ecological Marxism, defending Marx's political economy against charges of Promethean productivism.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, value-theory, Burkett, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@book{saito2017ecosocialism,
  author = {Saito, Kohei},
  title = {Karl Marx's Ecosocialism: Capital, Nature, and the Unfinished Critique of Political Economy},
  publisher = {Monthly Review Press},
  year = {2017},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctt1gk099m},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on Marx's unpublished natural-science notebooks (excerpts from Liebig and other agricultural chemists, published only in the ongoing Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe), Saito argues that Marx's ecological thought did not stop developing with the metabolic-rift passages of Capital but deepened continuously after 1868, as Marx pursued an increasingly systematic "theory of metabolism" intended, but never completed, as part of his critique of political economy. The book's two parts trace, first, how "alienation of nature" is built into Marx's theory of modernity, and second, how the MEGA2 notebooks document this unfinished extension.

  RELEVANCE: This is the philological anchor for the domain's central claim that historical materialism's treatment of nature is recoverable and extendable rather than a fixed, exhausted nineteenth-century doctrine; its argument that Marx's late notebooks revise rather than confirm a productivist "primacy of the productive forces" bears directly on the analytical-Marxist reconstruction of historical materialism covered elsewhere in this review.

  POSITION: MEGA2-based philological reconstruction of Marx's ecology as an unfinished, still-developing component of the critique of political economy.
  },
  sep_context = {SEP's "Karl Marx" entry (section "6 Alternative Interpretations") cites this book as evidence for a first stage of Marx's retreat from productivism: "in the period following the publication of Volume One of Capital, Marx increasingly thought of communism in eco-socialist terms, as offering sustainable economic growth (Saito 2017)."},
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, metabolic-rift, MEGA, Saito, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@book{saito2023anthropocene,
  author = {Saito, Kohei},
  title = {Marx in the Anthropocene: Towards the Idea of Degrowth Communism},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  year = {2023},
  doi = {10.1017/9781108933544},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Saito argues, again via the MEGA2 notebooks (including Marx's 1881 correspondence with Vera Zasulich on the Russian obshchina), that in his final years Marx abandoned the developmental, productive-forces-expanding vision of communism attributed to him by orthodox historical materialism and moved toward "degrowth communism": a vision of post-capitalist society organized around sustainable, non-expansionary use-value production rather than continued growth of exchange-value and productive capacity.

  RELEVANCE: This is the domain's most direct challenge to Cohen's "primacy thesis" (the claim that historical materialism explains social change via the tendency of productive forces to develop and outgrow existing relations of production): if Saito's reading is correct, Marx himself came to doubt that further development of the productive forces is either historically inevitable or emancipatory, which would require reconstructing rather than merely defending the classical theory of history's technological core.

  POSITION: Most far-reaching ecological-Marxist revision of historical materialism's productive-forces thesis, read as textual development internal to Marx's own late thought.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, degrowth, MEGA, Saito, primacy-thesis, High},
  abstract = {Facing global climate crisis, Karl Marx's ecological critique of capitalism more clearly demonstrates its importance than ever. This book explains why Marx's ecology had to be marginalized and even suppressed by Marxists after his death throughout the twentieth century. Marx's ecological critique of capitalism, however, revives in the Anthropocene against dominant productivism and monism. Investigating new materials published in the complete works of Marx and Engels (Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe), Saito offers a wholly novel idea of Marx's alternative to capitalism that should be adequately characterized as degrowth communism. This provocative interpretation of the late Marx sheds new lights on the recent debates on the relationship between society and nature and invites readers to envision a post-capitalist society without repeating the failure of the actually existing socialism of the twentieth century.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{moore2017metabolicshift,
  author = {Moore, Jason W.},
  title = {Metabolic Rift or Metabolic Shift? Dialectics, Nature, and the World-Historical Method},
  journal = {Theory and Society},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {46},
  number = {4},
  pages = {285--318},
  doi = {10.1007/s11186-017-9290-6},
  abstract = {In the flowering of Red-Green Thought over the past two decades, metabolic rift thinking is surely one of its most colorful varieties. The metabolic rift has captured the imagination of critical environmental scholars, becoming a shorthand for capitalism's troubled relations in the web of life. This article pursues an entwined critique and reconstruction: of metabolic rift thinking and the possibilities for a post-Cartesian perspective on historical change, the world-ecology conversation. Far from dismissing metabolic rift thinking, my intention is to affirm its dialectical core. At stake is not merely the mode of explanation within environmental sociology. The impasse of metabolic rift thinking is suggestive of wider problems across the environmental social sciences, now confronted by a double challenge. One of course is the widespread -- and reasonable -- sense of urgency to evolve modes of thought appropriate to an era of deepening biospheric instability. The second is the widely recognized -- but inadequately internalized -- understanding that humans are part of nature.},
  abstract_source = {core},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Moore argues that "metabolic rift" thinking, despite its dialectical intentions, tacitly reproduces a Cartesian nature/society dualism -- treating "nature" as an external object acted upon by an internal "society" -- and proposes instead a "world-ecology" method that theorizes capitalism as a way of organizing nature (capitalism-in-nature) rather than a social system that merely disrupts an external nature. He offers this as a "critique and reconstruction," not an outright rejection, of the rift tradition.

  RELEVANCE: This is the domain's central internal challenge to the Foster/Burkett/Saito metabolic-rift program, reopening -- in ecological terms -- historical materialism's long-standing base/superstructure and structure/agency dualisms; it is the direct target of Malm's "Against Hybridism" defense of dualist historical materialism included in this domain.

  POSITION: World-ecology critique of metabolic-rift dualism, proposing a monist alternative in which capital and nature are co-produced rather than externally related.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, world-ecology, metabolic-rift-debate, Moore, High}
}

@incollection{gellert2018distinctions,
  author = {Gellert, Paul K.},
  title = {Bunker's Ecologically Unequal Exchange, Foster's Metabolic Rift, and Moore's World-Ecology: Distinctions With or Without a Difference?},
  booktitle = {Ecologically Unequal Exchange},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {107--140},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-319-89740-0_5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gellert compares three ecological-Marxist frameworks -- Stephen Bunker's ecologically unequal exchange, Foster's metabolic rift, and Moore's world-ecology -- asking whether their disagreements track substantively different causal mechanisms and units of analysis or are largely terminological restatements of a shared underlying diagnosis of capitalism's ecological destructiveness.

  RELEVANCE: This is the domain's synthetic overview of the live metabolic-rift/world-ecology controversy, giving the review a secondary, adjudicating vantage point on the Foster/Saito vs. Moore dispute rather than relying solely on the disputants' own framings (Foster, Saito, Moore, Malm); it also links this domain back to the world-systems/dependency-theory domain via Bunker's unequal-exchange tradition.

  POSITION: Comparative/adjudicating overview of the metabolic-rift, world-ecology, and unequal-exchange traditions within ecological Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, metabolic-rift-debate, unequal-exchange, synthesis, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{malm2019hybridism,
  author = {Malm, Andreas},
  title = {Against Hybridism: Why We Need to Distinguish between Nature and Society, Now More than Ever},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {27},
  number = {2},
  pages = {156--187},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-00001610},
  abstract = {It is fashionable to argue that nature and society are obsolete categories. The two, we are told, can no longer be distinguished from one another; continuing loyalty to the 'binary' of the natural and the social blinds us to the logic of current ecological crises. This article outlines an argument for the opposite position: now more than ever -- particularly in our rapidly warming world -- we need to sift out the social components from the natural, if we wish to understand the crises and retain the possibility of intervening in them. Tracing the current of hybridism to the writings of Bruno Latour, this article ends with a critique of the foremost proponent of a hybridism in Marxist garb: Jason W. Moore. Against his theories, it suggests that historical materialism is a form of property dualism that distinguishes between social and natural relations while considering them equally material in substance. That is also the analytical premise of ecological class hatred, the flames of which ecological Marxism seeks to fan.},
  abstract_source = {semantic_scholar},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Malm defends a dualist reading of historical materialism -- nature and society are really distinct kinds of relations, though equally material -- against "hybridist" positions (traced to Bruno Latour) that dissolve the nature/society distinction. He argues Moore's world-ecology is hybridism "in Marxist garb," and that abandoning the distinction undermines the analytical and political work of identifying capital's specific responsibility for ecological crisis.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most explicit contemporary defense of historical materialism's ontological commitments (a "property dualism" of social and natural relations) against a rival ecological-Marxist current, directly engaging Moore's critique included in this domain and showing how metaphysical commitments inherited from classical historical materialism remain contested terrain in current ecological theory.

  POSITION: Dualist defense of historical materialism against Latourian/world-ecology hybridism.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, metabolic-rift-debate, dualism, Malm, High}
}

@book{malm2016fossilcapital,
  author = {Malm, Andreas},
  title = {Fossil Capital: The Rise of Steam Power and the Roots of Global Warming},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {2016},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Malm offers a historical-materialist account of the transition from water to coal-fired steam power in the early British cotton industry, arguing against standard "energy availability" explanations that steam was adopted not because coal was cheaper or more abundant than water power but because it gave capitalists greater control over labor -- steam plants could be sited in labor markets rather than at fixed watercourse locations. He terms the resulting socio-technical formation "fossil capital," locating the origin of anthropogenic climate change in specifically capitalist relations of production and class struggle over labor discipline, not in technology or population growth as such.

  RELEVANCE: This is a sustained empirical application of historical-materialist method (relations of production and class struggle explaining a landmark technological transition) to the origins of the fossil economy, making the case that climate change itself is a class-specific rather than species-general ("Anthropocene") phenomenon -- directly relevant to how this domain's ecological Marxism reconstructs historical materialism's causal explanatory core.

  POSITION: Historical-materialist, class-struggle explanation of the fossil-fuel transition, contesting the "Anthropocene" framing of climate change as insufficiently attentive to capitalist class relations.
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, fossil-capital, Malm, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{chibber2011living,
  author = {Chibber, Vivek},
  title = {What Is Living and What Is Dead in the Marxist Theory of History},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {19},
  number = {2},
  pages = {60--91},
  doi = {10.1163/156920611X573798},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chibber examines two influential attempts (by Erik Olin Wright and colleagues, and by Alan Carling) to rescue G.A. Cohen's classical, determinist reconstruction of the Marxist theory of history from its acknowledged weaknesses, and argues these rescue attempts are largely unsuccessful; to the extent they do render the theory more plausible, he shows, they do so only by converting it, willy-nilly, into a class-struggle theory. Chibber concludes this spells the demise of the classical (technologically determinist) version of historical materialism, while insisting this does not leave a merely voluntaristic account of history in its place.

  RELEVANCE: This paper directly addresses the domain's central question of how historical materialism has been reconstructed after Cohen's own primacy-thesis debates left the classical theory in doubt, situating the ecological-Marxist revisions covered elsewhere in this domain within a broader post-Cohen effort to specify a defensible, non-technologically-determinist core for historical materialism.

  POSITION: Post-Cohen assessment concluding that the classical, determinist theory of history cannot be rescued, but that a class-struggle-centered materialism survives its collapse.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism-defense, post-Cohen, Chibber, High},
  abstract = {Abstract During the 1980s and 1990s, the debate on the Marxist theory of history centred largely around the work of Robert Brenner's property-relations-centred construal of it, and G.A. Cohen's attempt to revive the classical, determinist argument. This article examines two influential arguments by Erik Wright and his colleagues, and by Alan Carling, which acknowledge important weaknesses in Cohen's work, but which also try to construct a more plausible version of his theory. I show that the attempts to rescue Cohen are largely unsuccessful. And, to the extent that they render the argument plausible, they do so at the cost of turning it, willy-nilly, into a kind of class-struggle theory. I conclude that this spells the demise of the classical version of historical materialism, but also observe that this does not leave us with a voluntaristic understanding of history, as some of its defenders fear.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{chibber2013postcolonial,
  author = {Chibber, Vivek},
  title = {Postcolonial Theory and the Specter of Capital},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {2013},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chibber defends, against Subaltern Studies and postcolonial theory, the claim that capital has genuine "universalizing tendencies" -- that capitalist competition compels structurally similar forms of labor discipline, state formation, and class conflict across colonial and postcolonial social formations -- and argues that postcolonial theorists' insistence on the radical cultural and historical difference of the non-Western world rests on mischaracterizations of both Marx's theory and the actual history of bourgeois revolutions in the West.

  RELEVANCE: This is the domain's most polemical contemporary defense of historical materialism's universalizing ambitions in a changed political and academic climate (the ascendancy of postcolonial theory in the humanities), extending the world-systems/dependency-theory domain's concern with Eurocentrism into a direct confrontation with post-Marxist and postcolonial critiques of historical-materialist categories.

  POSITION: Defense of historical materialism's universalizing categories (capital, class, competition) against postcolonial-theoretical claims of Western/non-Western civilizational difference.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism-defense, postcolonial-theory, Chibber, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{lazarus2016spectre,
  author = {Lazarus, Neil},
  title = {Vivek Chibber and the Spectre of Postcolonial Theory},
  journal = {Race \& Class},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {57},
  number = {3},
  pages = {88--106},
  doi = {10.1177/0306396815611853},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lazarus argues that Chibber's "Postcolonial Theory and the Specter of Capital" makes a valuable materialist contribution -- particularly its demonstration that capital's "universalizing tendency" need not entail cultural homogenization or the progressive spread of "Western" norms -- but that the book's polemical tone and its conflation of the historiographic project of Subaltern Studies with "postcolonial theory" more broadly cause it to misrepresent both traditions and their institutional relationship, weakening its own defense of historical-materialist universalism. Lazarus further argues Chibber wrongly diagnoses postcolonial theory as "Eurocentric" when it is better understood as a conjuncturally distinct version of "Third Worldism."

  RELEVANCE: This paper documents that Chibber's defense of historical materialism against postcolonial critique remains a live and unsettled controversy rather than a closed case, giving the review direct evidence of an ongoing, unresolved contemporary debate about how far historical-materialist categories can be defended against charges of Eurocentrism.

  POSITION: Sympathetic-critical response to Chibber, arguing his defense of universalizing capital succeeds on some points but overreaches and misdiagnoses its target on others.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism-defense, postcolonial-theory, Chibber-debate, Lazarus, Medium},
  abstract = {In this critical assessment of Vivek Chibber's Postcolonial Theory and the Specter of Capital, the author argues that the book contributes appreciably to the materialist critique of postcolonial studies, but that its effect is weakened both by its tone and its failure to identify its object precisely enough. In conflating the historiographic project of 'Subaltern Studies' with 'postcolonial theory', Chibber misrepresents the histories of each of these formations and also the institutional relationship between them. He is at his most compelling in his commentary on capital's 'universalising tendency', in which he demonstrates that arguments about capitalist globalisation do not require one to anticipate an increased homogenisation of the world or, more narrowly still, the progressive universalisation of 'western' norms, values and modes of social life. His critique of the deep-set postcolonialist conviction as to the radical difference and incommensurability between 'western' and 'non-western' worlds is also very valuable. But because he does not utilise the full resources of Marxist and anti-imperialist theory -- above all, the theory of uneven and combined development -- Chibber fails to diagnose 'postcolonial theory' for what it is: a conjuncturally distinct version of 'Third Worldism'. He wrongly accuses it, instead, of 'Eurocentrism'.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{fosterburkett2016antique,
  author = {Foster, John Bellamy and Burkett, Paul},
  title = {Marx and the Earth: An Anti-Critique},
  booktitle = {Marx and the Earth: An Anti-Critique},
  publisher = {Brill},
  year = {2016},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004288799},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Foster and Burkett respond systematically to roughly a decade of criticism of the metabolic-rift program (including charges that it lapses into naturalistic determinism, mishandles the nature/society relation, or is superseded by Moore's world-ecology), defending the coherence of Marx's own ecological value theory and the metabolic-rift reading of Capital by elaborating the roles of dialectics, open-system thermodynamics, intrinsic value, and aesthetic understanding in Marx's original critique.

  RELEVANCE: This "anti-critique" shows the metabolic-rift program actively defending and refining itself in response to the same world-ecology and hybridism challenges (Moore, and by extension the concerns Malm later addresses) treated elsewhere in this domain, demonstrating that the rift/world-ecology dispute is a genuinely two-sided, ongoing exchange rather than a one-sided critique.

  POSITION: Sustained self-defense of the metabolic-rift program against a decade of ecological-Marxist and other criticism, proposing a "new red-green synthesis."
  },
  keywords = {ecological-marxism, metabolic-rift-debate, Foster, Burkett, Medium},
  abstract = {A decade and a half ago John Bellamy Foster and Paul Burkett introduced a new, revolutionary understanding of the ecological foundations of Marx's thought, demonstrating that Marx's concepts of the universal metabolism of nature, social metabolism, and metabolic rift prefigured much of modern systems ecology. Ecological relations were shown to be central to Marx's critique of capitalism, including his value analysis. Now in Marx and the Earth Foster and Burkett expand on this analysis in the process of responding to recent ecosocialist criticisms of Marx. The result is a full-fledged anti-critique -- pointing to the crucial roles that dialectics, open-system thermodynamics, intrinsic value, and aesthetic understandings played in the original Marxian critique, holding out the possibility of a new red-green synthesis.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{prewetal2018enduringrelevance,
  author = {Prew, Paul and Rotta, Tomás and Smith, Tony and Vidal, Matt},
  title = {The Enduring Relevance of Karl Marx},
  booktitle = {The Oxford Handbook of Karl Marx},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {3--34},
  doi = {10.1093/oxfordhb/9780190695545.013.51},
  abstract = {This chapter is the introduction to The Oxford Handbook of Karl Marx. It summarizes Marx's enduring relevance by demonstrating the continuing applicability of his concepts and theories to understanding twenty-first century capitalism and its crises, along with the historical development of human society across varying modes of production. It presents an intellectual biography linking the major moments in Marx's life to his ideas and theories. The biography also gives insight into Marx's approach to research by focusing more closely on the method he outlined in the Grundrisse. It demonstrates, among other things, that Marx continually revised his ideas in light of new evidence and argumentation. The chapter concludes with brief summaries of the handbook's contributions, paying specific attention to the ongoing relevance of each chapter to societal concerns.},
  abstract_source = {core},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Introducing a seven-part, multi-disciplinary handbook (sociology, economics, political science, philosophy, history, geography, and ecology), the editors argue that Marx's foundational categories -- including historical materialism, class, capital, value, crisis, and ideology -- continue to be "defended, elaborated and applied" in contemporary empirical social science, against repeated "obituaries" declaring Marxism's death; the handbook's Foundations section is explicitly devoted to defenses of these categories, and later sections extend them to core, peripheral, and semi-peripheral accumulation, crisis, and class struggle.

  RELEVANCE: This is the domain's synthetic, retrospective statement of where the historical-materialism debate stands across the social sciences in the late 2010s: a scholarly consensus among contributing specialists that the theory's core commitments remain live research programs rather than settled or refuted doctrine, providing this review's contemporary bookend against which the ecological and postcolonial-theory controversies (also covered in this domain) can be read as instances of exactly this kind of ongoing defense-and-elaboration.

  POSITION: Editorial/synthetic overview asserting the continued defensibility and productivity of Marx's (including historical-materialist) categories across the social sciences.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism-defense, synthesis, handbook, High}
}
